A little background.  I've been playing Nethack & its spawn (though not
its ancestors/cousins; I've tried, didn't like any of them) since 1991.
 I have ascended exactly once, and I'd hardly call it that since I
save-file cheat constantly, without which I'd barely make it past DL3.

I know that many/most/all of the folks here play to meet the challenge
of the game and see perma-death as a key aspect of it.  To a great
extent I agree with that in principle.  In fact, I struggle to define
exactly what it is that keeps me coming back to the game given that I
am clearly personally unable to meet that particular challenge.  Over
the years I've played scores of games of various sorts, from arcade to
modern MMORPGs to console to whatever.  Nethack is absolutely the only
game (not counting Tetris :) ) that I have returned to over and over
again, regardless of what else I might be playing at any given time.

The sad truth is that I'm really quite terrible at pretty much all
games that don't involve playing cards (of the
hearts-spades-clubs-diamonds variety, that is) or falling blocks.
Whatever it is that makes people good at them, I ain't got.  Certainly
a part of that is that I really don't care very much about the goals as
defined by the developers of the games I am playing.

Oddly, that includes Nethack.  And, even if I do care about the goal, I
have some sort of innate ROI calculator as to what I'm going to have to
do to achieve it.  As an example, I currently have a character who's
just obtained the Amulet in vanilla and who (via save-file cheating, of
course, otherwise she'd be dead roughly eleventy-million times over by
now) is prepared to head back up to the Planes.  I stopped playing her.
 I stopped playing her because I knew I'd have to
die-reload-save-die-reload-save a zillion times, continue killing
*(&W$%ing Rodney every two seconds which is unappealing even though she
has Finger of Death not to mention a horde of pets that barely fit in
the DLs due to charm monster, and continue the aggravating task of
managing inventory which grows rapidly out of control even with
gauntlets of power & a blessed bag of holding.  And the alternative to
cheating is not only to die before Minetown every single time, but also
to be utterly unable to do the kind of exploration and experimentation
that is part of what keeps me coming back.  Try potions? Not on your
life.  Read scrolls? Terrible idea.  Spend turn after turn after turn
attempting to identify items via drudgery and esoteric methods?
Absolutely.

A huge part of what keeps me playing is the unbelievable depth of
thought in the game.  I have repeatedly recommended Nethack to other
people as the game I consider to be the shining example of brilliant
game design, with far-reaching ramifications, considered consequences,
a phenomenal array of interactions with pretty much everything in the
game, and such a complex experience set that spoilers can hardly be
called spoilers.

I live and die (mostly die) by the spoilers.  As I play I hop back and
forth between the spoilers and the game constantly, and by this point I
know half the answers myself.  And yet the game still manages to
surprise me at points.  But that knowledge has also narrowed the game
significantly for me.  I don't like to play a character that doesn't
start the game or obtain within a few levels some specific items (charm
monster is WAY up on that list.) I think that the roles could use some
additions, even with SLASH'EM.  I don't like to play fighter-type
classes because a., they're just not my style, and b., I become rapidly
frustrated because I want to be able to use spells that I know I would
have as a mage, so I invariably only like to play the Wizard role -
high enough INT that I can cast decent spells early on and more
randomly-assigned starter objects so I usually wind up with at least
SOMEthing I want when I first start the game (as opposed to other magey
roles, including the mage classes in SLASH"EM.)

My point being that I think there is a lot to Nethack & its children
that can be appreciated by someone that is unwilling or unable to stick
to permadeath. I think this is especially true now that there are
several graphically-attractive versions.  I know that there are many
die-hards that would sooner eat Rodney's corpse than play a baby
version, which is both entirely respectable and impressive.  There's
also only one person to whom I've recommended the game that actually
managed to play for any length of time - everyone else finds it too
frustrating or irritating and doesn't want to do all the research
necessary to experience the joys of the game that they have only heard
about.

If I could write C, I'd do it myself, but I can't, and I won't have
time (or, probably, inclination or talent - I'm like the world's worst
programmer) to learn anytime this decade.  I think that a version that
made the game more accessible would not need huge changes. I also think
that this could get more people to *eventually* graduate to the
"grown-up" version of the game. I'm sure others have proposed this
before me, but what the hell, right?

For a baby version, these are some of the things I can think of off the
top of my head:

- Obviously, saving-enabling the game so you can "save as" and without
having to dupe your save file elsewhere.

- "Baby" roles. If initial roles were used to select a game mode,
there's no real reason that a baby version and a grown-up version
couldn't be in the same package.  I'd see these roles as having more of
the (unbalanceable in any "grownup" game) tank-mage concept behind
them.  Baby wizard, baby knight, etc.

- A pool of items that were weighted into the first three levels of the
game with randomly-selected members of that pool showing up (meaning,
say, 2 out of 10 items would always appear within those first three
levels, which 2 would be randomly selected), with the pool consisting
of objects that are extremely helpful and, one might say at least for
some of them, necessary - bags of holding, wands of wishing, charm
monster, the usual.

- Restrictions placed on highly problematic consequences during the
early part of the game, or increased chances of remedies for those
consequences.  I think discovering the consequences of certain actions
is a big part of the enjoyment of the game, but for a new player it's
too frustrating to constantly run up against things like scrolls of
punishment, amnesia, food poisoning (especially since it's awfully hard
to keep oneself fed many times), and cursed items without having any
recourse to fix the situation, and the original problem begets
nervousness about experimentation, so if you read a scroll of
punishment first, you probably won't try to read the rest of the
scrolls in your pack (without cheating, anyway), so even if you have
remove curse, you won't find that out.

- Increased chances of positive consequences for interesting
experiments, especially early on in the game.  Sitting on thrones,
quaffing from fountains, kicking sinks - for a new player, the fact
that those things are at all possible is one of the things that makes
the game so interesting.  Being punished for that kind of exploration,
especially early on, makes it impossible for the player to then enjoy
that exploration.  Get bashed by a water demon once, and you're a lot
less likely to quaff from fountains, which kind of sucks.  Things like
quaffing from fountains are one of the things that, IMHO, make the game
so compelling.  Too much punishment for experimentation early on will
cause a lot of people to drop the game entirely.

Anyway, just some thoughts from a very, very bad and very, very loyal
Nethack player.  These are the things that I cheat in order to be able
to do.  I spend a HUGE amount of time in the game trying to play by its
rules to avoid negative consequences - I'll stand around for a long
time to see if a pet will walk on something before I put it on, for
example - and it still isn't enough to get me very far at all.  I get
frustrated that even with the many precautions I take, I am constantly
in trouble.  I'd enjoy taking those precautions - and I'd probably take
more precautions - if I knew that I was not that likely to get
slaughtered for something silly three turns later.

